WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.543
A primary database can stream changes to read
replicas Each replica maintains a copy of the
dataset

00:00:06.543 --> 00:00:12.294
and can answer eligible reads, so many read requests
no longer compete for the primary.

00:00:12.294 --> 00:00:18.507
Only if its consistency policy ensures it has the
required write; otherwise use an appropriate

00:00:18.507 --> 00:00:20.489
primary or session-aware read.

00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.998
Sharding assigns disjoint subsets of rows to
different database partitions A useful key places
the

00:00:26.998 --> 00:00:32.037
data for a common operation together while
distributing storage and traffic.

00:00:32.037 --> 00:00:32.236
No.

00:00:32.236 --> 00:00:38.667
Each feed may still contact every shard, and the
coordinator must merge results; skew and network

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:39.927
cost can also grow.
